    "body": "<!-- pr-reviewer: 95a70b16a6e79d63e2935ad0cafb9b86d7569c4e -->\n**Review (M3): the code is clean, but this needs a maintainer hand check.** The Z1's posted G-code has never been run on a real machine, the Hub or a simulator.\n\n**What looks good:**\n- It's a small data PR: one machine file plus its registry line. It's in mm and of kind mill, so `post_serves` accepts the Carvera post.\n- The sources are public (makera.com, cnx-software). Where the figures conflict, the decision file keeps the lower one and separates what is confirmed from what is assumed.\n- Tests pin the figures, the M7/M9 coolant codes, and the named refusals for X overtravel and the spindle ceiling.\n\n**Local results** (merged with main, which merges cleanly):\n- `makera_z1`: 4 passed.\n- `cargo xtask gate quick`: green.\n- Clippy is clean.\n- Mutation checks on the `.mch` were all caught: X/Y travel 200→250, spindle 13000→15000 rpm, M7→M8, Z travel 100→150.\n\n**Requested before merge:**\n1. **Hand check.** Nothing published confirms that the Z1 speaks the carvera.cps dialect. Read the posted program (G21, `M6 Tn`, M7/M9) in the Hub, and ideally cut an air pass on a Z1. The Z1 has no tool changer, so how it handles `M6 Tn` is the most likely thing to go wrong.\n\n**Notes:**\n- Z overtravel has no refusing test; a Z mutation is caught only by the listing test. A deep-face case would pin it.\n- The travel limits are the full stroke either side of work zero. That admits a program spanning up to twice the physical stroke. This matches the Carvera files and is documented.\n- The 13000 rpm and 1200 mm/min figures come from a third-party review, not from Makera.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
